Proposed — NOT APPLIED. Two small changes to app/strategy/scoring_lane.py.

Neither touches a cap, a gate, a plan, a price, or the amount spent. The lane
buys exactly the same finals before and after. What changes is what the cycle
SAYS about them.

Evidence: docs/SCORING_STOP_DIAGNOSIS.md (production, 2026-09-19 UTC).


================================================================================
FIX 1 — a user who met their target is not "short"
================================================================================

`allowance()` tests the cost ceiling BEFORE the delivered branch, so a Free user
who received 26 jobs against a target of 20 returns

    daily cost ceiling (140/120 finals) at 26/20 delivered

which does not start with "delivered" and is therefore filed under
`plan_capped_users` and warned about as "stopped SHORT of their day's shortlist
target". It fired every 30 minutes for 13 hours on a day the user got 130% of
their plan. The reason string already carries the evidence; only the
classification is wrong.

Keying on the DELIVERED NUMBERS rather than on the prefix of a sentence also
removes the string-matching fragility — `allow.reason.startswith("delivered")`
is one reworded f-string away from silently reclassifying every user.

--- a/app/strategy/scoring_lane.py
+++ b/app/strategy/scoring_lane.py
@@ ~1313, the work-list loop @@
         allow = _finals_allowance(uid, settings.scoring_per_user_cap)
         if allow.n <= 0:
-            if allow.reason.startswith("delivered"):
+            # "Did this user get their day's jobs?" is a question about the
+            # numbers, not about how the reason sentence happens to open. A
+            # ceiling stop AT or PAST the target is a user who received what
+            # the plan promises and then ran out of budget to look further —
+            # success, not the Aug 14-21 stall this warning exists for.
+            _delivered, _target = _delivery_position(uid)
+            if allow.reason.startswith("delivered") or (
+                    _target > 0 and _delivered >= _target):
                 delivered_out += 1
             else:
                 capped_out += 1

with, next to _plan_budget:

+def _delivery_position(uid) -> tuple[int, int]:
+    """(delivered today, the plan's daily target). (0, 0) when unknowable —
+    which keeps the OLD classification, so an unresolvable plan can never
+    silence the warning."""
+    try:
+        from app.matching.finals_budget import delivered_today
+        _ceiling, target = _plan_budget(uid)
+        return delivered_today(uid), int(target or 0)
+    except Exception:
+        return 0, 0

`delivered_today` is cached (20s) and `_plan_budget` was already called for
this user in `_finals_allowance` on the same pass, so this adds no query on
the hot path.


================================================================================
FIX 2 — name the stop in the cycle stats
================================================================================

`plan_capped_users` is a bare count. The reason string is built with the numbers
in it and then spent on a per-user `log.debug` (scoring_lane.py:1320) that
production does not run. Identifying which of the four stops fired took a
reconstruction across three lanes' log lines and a process-cumulative token
counter. One key ends that.

--- a/app/strategy/scoring_lane.py
+++ b/app/strategy/scoring_lane.py
@@ module level, next to _last_capped_log @@
+# The stops a user can hit, as a FIXED vocabulary. `plan_capped_users` counts
+# how many users stopped short; this says which stop each one hit. The reason
+# string itself carries live counts ("140/120 finals"), so it must never become
+# a stats key — that would give every cycle's stats a different shape.
+_STOP_BUCKETS = (
+    ("daily cost ceiling", "cost_ceiling"),
+    ("yield ",             "yield_collapsed"),
+    ("anthropic prescore", "prescore_allowance"),
+    ("slate full",         "slate_full"),
+)
+
+
+def _stop_bucket(reason: str) -> str:
+    for prefix, name in _STOP_BUCKETS:
+        if reason.startswith(prefix):
+            return name
+    return "other"
+

@@ the work-list loop @@
     capped_out = 0
     delivered_out = 0
+    stop_reasons: dict = {}
     ...
             else:
                 capped_out += 1
+                _b = _stop_bucket(allow.reason)
+                stop_reasons[_b] = stop_reasons.get(_b, 0) + 1

@@ where the counters are published @@
     if capped_out:
         stats["plan_capped_users"] = capped_out
+        stats["plan_capped_reasons"] = stop_reasons
     if delivered_out:
         stats["target_met_users"] = delivered_out


================================================================================
BLAST RADIUS
================================================================================

tests/test_adaptive_finals_budget.py pins the taxonomy with two cases:

  * "delivered 35/35 — the day's jobs are on the board"  -> target_met_users
    Still target_met: the prefix branch is kept and checked first.
  * "daily cost ceiling (250/250 finals) at 9/35 delivered" -> plan_capped_users
    Still capped: 9 < 35, so the new numeric branch does not fire. Note the
    fixture stubs `_finals_allowance`, so `_delivery_position` reads the real
    (empty) tables and returns delivered=0 — which also keeps it capped.

  * `"plan_capped_users" not in stats` in the delivered case — unaffected;
    `plan_capped_reasons` is only written inside `if capped_out:`.

A test for the new case (ceiling stop AT the target -> target_met_users, no
"stopped SHORT" warning) should land with FIX 1.


================================================================================
NOT PROPOSED HERE
================================================================================

Free's `finals_daily` (120) is below what Free's own `shortlist_daily` (20)
costs at the measured 6.5 finals per delivered job (~130). That is why the
ceiling stop is the one that always fires on Free, and why challenge mode —
which lives behind the delivered branch — can never run for a Free user.

Changing either number is a pricing decision, not a bug fix, and was explicitly
out of scope for this investigation. Flagged in docs/SCORING_STOP_DIAGNOSIS.md
so it is decided deliberately rather than discovered again.
